From ed47898c6878fe09c4372e84073180f9ce896bfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:38:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 43/45] feat(scripting): effect material properties run their
 scripts and animations

An image effect's shader constants carry scripts and keyframes of their
own: a tint cycling through the rainbow, god rays following the music, a
media widget's play button taking the accent colour. None of them ran;
the passes drew the static value from scene.json.

Each image now registers its effects with the script engine. Every pass
constant that carries a script is loaded as a property script, and every
one with a timeline is played with the layer's own animations. The
passes bind their uniforms to the same values, so what a script returns
or a curve plays is what the shader draws.

A script on an effect property sees the effect as thisObject (IEffect),
as Wallpaper Engine binds it, and the layer as thisLayer. IEffect has:
- name and visible;
- the effect's material properties by name, read and written, a write
  reaching every pass that has the property;
- getAnimation (name?), with no name the animation on the property the
  script drives;
- setMaterialProperty (name, value).

Binding thisObject to the layer instead would let a media widget's
`thisObject.color = shared.accentColor` recolour the whole layer.

Effect visibility stays as loaded: the passes are built once, from the
effects visible at load, so a script can read visible and write it, but
the effect is not added or removed while the wallpaper runs.

